{
  "id": "a1-20",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 26,
  "title": "Demonstratives and Determiners",
  "summary": "The words that point — this, that, such, which — and those that quantify — all, every, nobody. They are adjectives in their behaviour: they decline and they agree, and the learner who has ¶17 has most of them already.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "sis-tas",
      "number": "132",
      "heading": "Šis and Tas",
      "statement": "**Šis** points at what is near — *this*; **tas** at what is further off or already spoken of — *that*. Both decline and agree with their noun in gender, number and case, exactly as an adjective does (¶17). Their endings are largely the adjective endings; what has to be learnt is the short stem and the handful of forms that do not follow it.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "šis and tas, singular",
          "columns": [
            "case",
            "šis (m.)",
            "ši (f.)",
            "tas (m.)",
            "ta (f.)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "nominative",
              "cells": [
                "šis",
                "ši",
                "tas",
                "ta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "genitive",
              "cells": [
                "šio",
                "šios",
                "to",
                "tos"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "dative",
              "cells": [
                "šiam",
                "šiai",
                "tam",
                "tai"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "accusative",
              "cells": [
                "šį",
                "šią",
                "tą",
                "tą"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "instrumental",
              "cells": [
                "šiuo",
                "šia",
                "tuo",
                "ta"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "locative",
              "cells": [
                "šiame",
                "šioje",
                "tame",
                "toje"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "In the plural: *šie, šios* and *tie, tos* in the nominative; *šių, tų* in the genitive; *šiuos, šias* and *tuos, tas* in the accusative."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Šis butas didesnis negu tas.",
          "gloss": "This flat is bigger than that one."
        },
        {
          "target": "Neskaityk šios knygos.",
          "gloss": "Don't read this book.",
          "note": "genitive of negation, the demonstrative agreeing"
        },
        {
          "target": "Tame name gyvena mano brolis.",
          "gloss": "My brother lives in that house."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "adjective-agreement",
        "negation-genitive",
        "comparison-negu"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "anas",
      "number": "133",
      "heading": "Anas: the Further Demonstrative",
      "statement": "Lithuanian has a **third** degree of pointing that English lacks. **Anas** is *that one over there* — further off than *tas*, or the second of two already contrasted. It declines like *geras*, with no irregularity at all, and it is much the rarest of the three.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Šis namas naujas, o anas senas.",
          "gloss": "This house is new, and that one over there is old."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ne šitą, aną!",
          "gloss": "Not this one — that one!"
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "In speech *šitas* is often heard for *šis*; it is the colloquial form of the same word and declines like *geras*."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "sis-tas",
        "adjective-agreement"
      ],
      "core": false
    },
    {
      "id": "toks",
      "number": "134",
      "heading": "Toks: Such",
      "statement": "**Toks, tokia** is *such* or *of that kind*, and it is the answer to *koks?* (¶51) as *tas* is the answer to *kuris?*. Note the idiom **Koks tu geras!** — *how kind of you* — where Lithuanian uses the question word for an exclamation, and **toks** answers it.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Noriu tokios pat knygos.",
          "gloss": "I want the same sort of book."
        },
        {
          "target": "Toks oras man nepatinka.",
          "gloss": "I do not like weather like this."
        },
        {
          "target": "Koks gražus miestas!",
          "gloss": "What a beautiful city!"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "koks",
        "adjective-agreement"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "kuris",
      "number": "135",
      "heading": "Kuris: Which of Several",
      "statement": "**Kuris, kuri** asks *which one* out of a known set, where **koks** asks *what kind*. The distinction is real and English blurs it: *which book?* is *kuri knyga?* if the books are before you and *kokia knyga?* if you are asking what sort of book it was. **Kelintas** asks the position in an order — *the how-manieth* (¶82).",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Three question words compared",
          "columns": [
            "word",
            "asks for",
            "example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "koks",
              "cells": [
                "what kind",
                "Kokia tai knyga? — What sort of book is it?"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "kuris",
              "cells": [
                "which of several",
                "Kurią knygą skaitai? — Which book are you reading?"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "kelintas",
              "cells": [
                "position in order",
                "Kelinta valanda? — What time is it?"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Kuris autobusas važiuoja į centrą?",
          "gloss": "Which bus goes to the centre?"
        },
        {
          "target": "Kuriame aukšte gyveni?",
          "gloss": "Which floor do you live on?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "koks",
        "question-adverbs",
        "telling-the-hour"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "visas-kiekvienas",
      "number": "136",
      "heading": "Visas and Kiekvienas",
      "statement": "**Visas** in the singular is *the whole*; in the plural, **visi, visos**, it is *all*. **Kiekvienas** is *each, every*, and being a distributive it is always **singular**, however many people are meant — in this it agrees with English *every* and not with *all*.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Whole, all, each",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "number"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "visas",
              "cells": [
                "visą dieną — all day",
                "singular: the whole of it"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "visi",
              "cells": [
                "visi studentai — all the students",
                "plural"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "kiekvienas",
              "cells": [
                "kiekvienas studentas — every student",
                "always singular"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Vakar lijo visą dieną.",
          "gloss": "Yesterday it rained all day.",
          "note": "accusative of duration (¶83)"
        },
        {
          "target": "Visi jau grįžo.",
          "gloss": "They have all come back already."
        },
        {
          "target": "Kiekvieną savaitę einu į kursus.",
          "gloss": "Every week I go to the classes."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "adjective-number",
        "accusative-of-time",
        "particles"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "niekas-negation",
      "number": "137",
      "heading": "Niekas and the Doubled Negative",
      "statement": "**Niekas** is *nobody* and *nothing* together; its genitive **nieko** is met oftener than its nominative, because a negated verb governs the genitive (¶26). The rule that governs it is the one already given for *niekada* (¶41): a negative word requires the **verb to be negated as well**. Lithuanian doubles its negatives where English forbids it, and a single negative in such a sentence is simply wrong.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "The negative words and their doubling",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English",
            "the verb"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "nobody",
              "cells": [
                "Niekas neatėjo.",
                "Nobody came.",
                "negated"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "nothing",
              "cells": [
                "Nieko nematau.",
                "I see nothing.",
                "negated"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "never",
              "cells": [
                "Niekada nebuvau Kaune.",
                "I have never been to Kaunas.",
                "negated"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "nowhere",
              "cells": [
                "Niekur nevažiuoju.",
                "I am going nowhere.",
                "negated"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "Three negatives in one clause are perfectly ordinary: *Niekas niekada nieko nesakė* — nobody ever said anything."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Buvau parduotuvėje, bet nieko nepirkau.",
          "gloss": "I was at the shop but bought nothing."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mums nereikia nieko.",
          "gloss": "We need nothing."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "negation",
        "negation-genitive",
        "frequency-adverbs"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    },
    {
      "id": "tas-pats",
      "number": "138",
      "heading": "Tas Pats: the Same",
      "statement": "*The same* is **tas pats, ta pati** — literally *that itself* — and **both** words decline, each agreeing with the noun. English has one invariable word here and Lithuanian has two variable ones, so the phrase repays a moment's attention. Beside it stands **kitas**, *other* or *another* (¶103), which is its opposite.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "tas pats declined",
          "columns": [
            "case",
            "masculine",
            "feminine"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "nominative",
              "cells": [
                "tas pats",
                "ta pati"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "genitive",
              "cells": [
                "to paties",
                "tos pačios"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "dative",
              "cells": [
                "tam pačiam",
                "tai pačiai"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "accusative",
              "cells": [
                "tą patį",
                "tą pačią"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "locative",
              "cells": [
                "tame pačiame",
                "toje pačioje"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "The *č* of *paties, pačios* is the softening of *t* before a back vowel, met for the fourth time now (¶28, ¶95, ¶122)."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Mes gyvename tame pačiame name.",
          "gloss": "We live in the same house."
        },
        {
          "target": "Tai ta pati knyga.",
          "gloss": "That is the same book."
        },
        {
          "target": "Kitą savaitę važiuosime.",
          "gloss": "Next week we shall go."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "sis-tas",
        "adjective-agreement",
        "future-uses"
      ],
      "core": true,
      "footnotes": []
    }
  ],
